Notion provides a hosted [MCP (Model Context Protocol)](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/introduction) server that lets MCP clients work with Notion workspaces. Use Streamable HTTP at `https://mcp.notion.com/mcp`. If your client uses the deprecated `/sse` endpoint, [migrate to Streamable HTTP](/guides/mcp/get-started-with-mcp#migrate-from-the-legacy-sse-endpoint). Your client must support:
- OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code flow with PKCE
- Streamable HTTP (`/mcp`)
- Token refresh handling
- Secure credential storage

Discover the OAuth issuer and endpoints before redirecting the user. An unauthenticated request to `https://mcp.notion.com/mcp` returns a `401` with a `WWW-Authenticate` header. Its `resource_metadata` URL points to Notion’s [protected resource metadata](https://mcp.notion.com/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp). That document identifies the resource and its authorization server. The [authorization server metadata](https://mcp.notion.com/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server) lists the endpoints and supported authentication methods. The TypeScript SDK handles metadata discovery and issuer validation. This example is for Notion’s hosted endpoint.

## Step 7: Connect to MCP server with authentication
Use the v2 TypeScript client and select the July 2026 protocol. The SDK’s default mode uses the 2025 handshake. With the version pinned, the SDK calls `server/discover` and adds the July metadata and matching HTTP headers to subsequent requests.
```
import { Client, StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/client"
async function createMcpClient(accessToken: string): Promise<Client> {
const client = new Client(
{ name: "notion-workspace-client", version: "1.0.0" },
{ versionNegotiation: { mode: { pin: "2026-07-28" } } }
)
await client.connect(new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(mcpUrl, {
requestInit: { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}` } },
}))
return client
}
```

